application/DECISION-LOG.md:9:## 2026-11-04 (-23 r9) — a reissue or overpayment credit is not held to the payment family's cap
application/DECISION-LOG.md:11:- **`check_cash_source`'s family cap (a payment and its credits may never hold more than the payment brought in) does not apply to the two internal moves, a reissue and an overpayment credit; every other check on them stays (Ryan, 2026-11-04; option 1; Codex r8 B1).** A family can legitimately be over its payment: what was already paid out stands (K-10, 10-21). Codex's case: an escheated credit's refund comes back from the bank (60, against a 100 payment), Ryan's 10-21 rule requires the returned money be carried to a reissue, and the cap refused the reissue (110 > 100) though a reissue moves no money out of the family, so the return could not be recorded at all (confirmed, `tests/v5.4.2-23/review/codex-r8-probes/01-negative-pool-reissue.author-run.txt`). *Why:* the family sum already leaves out both moves, because each shifts money inside the family; the fix is as narrow as the fault. *Why not reshape the cap for every act to refuse only an act that adds to the overdraw (option 2):* more general, but it re-opens the check every reviewer has worked over in eight rounds, for cases no one has found. *Why not waive the reissue requirement while a family is over (option 3):* it reverses the 10-21 rule. *Tests:* Codex's 60 case in Q5; a one-cent external refund after the carry still refused; a mutation removing the skip. *Where it lives:* -23 round 9 (`check_cash_source`).
application/DECISION-LOG.md:13:## 2026-11-04 (-28 r2) — the fee's state-agency check keeps reading the bill's time zone; every Texas county is loaded once as shared data
application/DECISION-LOG.md:15:- **Keep the check as built (option 1): the pipeline safety fee's state-agency exemption reads the bill's time zone for every non-zero fee under an exempting rule; load all 254 Texas counties and their zones once, as platform data, in a patch of their own (-29) (Ryan, 2026-11-04).** Round 1 of -28 (Opus S3) found that only El Paso and Hudspeth have a zone loaded, so a fee anywhere else in Texas is refused (known limit L1), and proposed reading the zone only when the agency answer differs between the earliest and latest possible midnight. Ryan: counties and their zones almost never change; it is data the database should hold once for every customer. *Why:* `places` and `place_facts` are already shared by every utility and written only by reviewed platform migrations (-16), so the cost the alternative avoided was a one-time load we owe anyway, not onboarding work per utility; with every county present the simple check never refuses for missing data, and a missing zone can never hide until a rare bill. *Why not the two-midnight shortcut:* extra logic whose only purpose was to avoid that load. *What stays per utility:* each premise's county membership — address data the utility records, needed also for city-limits sales tax (Kyle 2026-11-03 Q4); a premise with none is still refused. *Why its own patch:* -28 is mid-review; nothing is live, so -29 follows it. *Where it lives:* -28 r2 (L1 restated, no code change), -29 (Census county list; zones per 49 CFR 71.7).
application/DECISION-LOG.md:17:## 2026-11-02 (-28 r1) — percentage rates are stored as a percent to four decimals; entered and shown however the utility writes them
application/DECISION-LOG.md:19:- **A percentage rate is stored in percent, to four decimals; the app accepts it in any form the clerk types (percent or fraction), converts it, and shows it in the utility's preferred form (Ryan, 2026-11-02).** Round 1 of -28 (Opus S2) found the four-decimal check refuses Texas's gas gross receipts tax (Tax Code §182.022: 0.581%, 1.070%, 1.997%) and eighth-of-a-percent sales-tax pieces when they are stored as fractions (unit `decimal`: 0.01997 needs five decimals), while the same rate in percent (1.997) is accepted. *Why:* the rule is about storage; four decimals of a percent is exact for every statutory rate seen, and one stored form gives one meaning to "four decimals". A small utility's clerk who has always written fractions must not be refused or made to convert by hand: the app does the conversion. *Why not let the fraction unit carry six decimals in storage:* two stored forms of the same number, and a schedule override often takes its unit from the base item in another table, so the check would need a trigger. *Why not leave clerks to enter percent:* a refusal they cannot act on. *Where it lives:* APPLICATION-CONTRACTS AC-45; -28's four-decimal check is unchanged, and its refusal on a `decimal`-unit rate says to store percentages in percent. Retiring the `decimal` unit is a later rates patch.
application/DECISION-LOG.md:21:## 2026-11-02 (-28 r1) — a consolidated bill is fixed once it goes out: every member bill final at issue, nothing attaches after (money stream's rule)
application/DECISION-LOG.md:23:- **A summary bill is issued only when every member bill is final, and no member bill attaches to it after it is issued (Ryan, 2026-11-02; option 1).** Round 1 of -28 (Codex B2, Opus N1, Fable N3) issued a consolidated parent while one child was still a draft, then moved that draft to an El Paso premise and attached a second child in another zone; both were accepted, and the parent's pipeline-safety fee, checked at issue, was afterwards refused on recheck. The schema already freezes an issued bill's own address and parent (`enforce_invoice_immutable`, CI-012), but per bill: nothing stops a summary going out over draft members, or a member joining a sent summary. *Why:* in practice a child's premise never moves (a service address is fixed) and a summary covers a known set of addresses; the reviewers' sequence is data entry no office does on purpose. Freezing the family at issue matches how a billing clerk already works, and it also keeps a sent summary's total equal to the bills it adds up, which matters more than the fee's day boundary. *Why not recheck the parent whenever a child changes (option 2):* a trigger on every invoice write to defend a state that should not exist, and it would refuse the child's write without saying why the family is closed. *Why not accept it (option 3):* the fee is small, but the same gap lets a summary's total drift from its members. *Where it lives:* invoices are the money stream's; sent as a board note (HANDOFF.md, rates → money, 2026-11-02). -28 states the gap as a known limit that the money stream's rule closes.
application/DECISION-LOG.md:25:## 2026-11-02 (-23 r8) — AC-44's netting: per-state before an escheat report; recorded as an application to the shortfall (-27)
application/DECISION-LOG.md:27:- **-27 is drafted right after -23 mirrors, ahead of -24 (Ryan, 2026-11-02).** The money stream's order becomes -23 → -27 → -24, with -25 and -26 (drafted, not reviewed) reviewed in -27's waits; then enrollments, notices, new rule types as before. *Why:* -27's row is a draw on a credit and a repayment on a payment, through -23's position and cap logic, which is freshest now and was just reviewed by the same three reviewers. *Why not keep it last:* by then that logic is cold, and -27 would likely take more rounds. *Cost:* -24 (unmatched cash, agency pledges) slips by -27's length; nothing in -24 depends on -27. -27 is a patch the 10-29 schema estimate (about 15 after -23) did not count: roughly one more. *Where it lives:* the board's patch table; `handoffs/schema-money.md` at wrap-up; the roadmap at wrap-up.
application/DECISION-LOG.md:28:- **Netting a credit against a bounced payment's shortfall is recorded as a new kind of application, pointed at the payment's shortfall, built as its own patch, v5.4.2-27 (Ryan, 2026-11-02; Opus r7 N3).** AC-44 called the netting "an ordinary application", but every application names an invoice, and the shortfall is on none: a bounce waits until the payment's bill applications are undone (those bills reopen and take a credit as usual), so what is left owed is money already paid out, chiefly a refund (Kyle's 10-21 example: 50 paid, 30 refunded, 50 bounced, 30 owed). *Why:* the row records that the credit's money paid the debt, so the credit reads 0 and its dormancy clock stops, the shortfall reads what is left, and the bank tie-out stays true; it also gives R24's never-closing credit a closing act. *Why not bill the shortfall as a charge, then apply to it:* Kyle (K-10, 10-21) rejected inventing a charge that never happened ("would look like a fee the customer was never assessed, which could cause trouble with the RRC and with customers"). *Why not record nothing (the core nets only in what it shows and allows):* the account balance is already right, but the credit keeps its money and its clock, the debt never reads paid, and the database still admits the credit's refund; a change in who holds the money is stored when it happens. *Why a follow-on patch, not round 8:* the new row is a draw on the credit and a repayment on the payment, through the position and cap logic that took -23 eight rounds; it is additive (no existing column changes meaning), so it costs no data migration later. *Where it lives:* APPLICATION-CONTRACTS AC-44; the board (-27 claimed, money stream).
application/DECISION-LOG.md:29:- **Netting across payments before an escheat report is a per-jurisdiction rule the core reads, not a fixed step (Ryan, 2026-11-02; Opus r7 N3).** AC-44 had told the core to apply a credit to what the customer owes on another payment before refunding *or escheating* it. Before a refund that stands: it is Kyle's one-pot ruling (10-30) and the utility's own policy. Before an escheat report it is a setoff against a separate debt, and whether a holder may make one before reporting is each state's unclaimed-property law. *Why:* a Texas launch never justifies Texas-only design; statutory rules go in per-jurisdiction rows (rule-terms convention, 10-06). *Why not always net:* where a state bars the setoff the core would under-report, and fixing that later means reworking core logic built on it. *Why not never net:* where a state allows it, the utility remits money it could lawfully keep against a real debt. *Default where no rule row exists:* report the credit's own money in full and collect the debt separately (the conservative side). *Not checked:* no statute has been read yet; filling each state's row is the work this creates, and the schema has no per-state escheat rule table today (dormancy is hard-coded at three years in a view). *Within one payment:* not a setoff (the same money coming back); the database nets it everywhere. *Cost now:* contract wording only, no schema change. *Where it lives:* APPLICATION-CONTRACTS AC-44.
application/DECISION-LOG.md:31:## 2026-11-02 (scenarios) — Ryan: the scenario cast is modelled on real small-city gas tariffs, under invented names
application/DECISION-LOG.md:33:- **Option 2 (Ryan, 2026-11-02).** The worked bills use a small cast in `scenarios/cast.md`: two city-owned systems (about 500 and about 5,000 meters) whose tariff structure (what is charged, how a rate change splits, deposits, late fees) and rounded rates come from real published Texas city gas tariffs, cited in the cast file, under invented names; one regulated utility as the case where Texas state rules bind; one invented non-Texas set of law rows, to show nothing is hard-coded. *Why:* Kyle checks bills that look like what our first customers send, and the rule questions a real tariff raises surface now rather than at onboarding. *Why not invented tariffs:* Kyle would check arithmetic on numbers nobody charges, and an unrealistic tariff hides rule questions. *Why not real names:* a scenario would then make claims about a real city's current policy, which goes stale and can be misread as the platform's view of that city. `application/places-sources/` covers the law but names no city's tariff, so the tariffs come from a web search. *Cost of changing later:* editing the cast file. *Where it lives:* `scenarios/cast.md` (to be written); the existing GBM fixture catalog is superseded.
application/DECISION-LOG.md:35:## 2026-11-02 (scenarios) — Ryan: one scenario is a rule line, Given / When / Then, and a worked bill
application/DECISION-LOG.md:37:- **The combined shape (Ryan, 2026-11-02).** Each scenario states the rule under test with its sources in one sentence; "Given" lists the facts, each with its source; "When" is the one event; "Then" is a worked bill (line, arithmetic, amount) plus any refusals or records expected; a last line names what is open and for whom. Shown on SCN-0001 (a mid-period rate change). The template is `scenarios/README.md`. *Why:* the scenarios have three readers — Kyle checks the table, Ryan checks the rule line, and a test is built straight from Given / When / Then. *Why not prose alone (May's choice):* the expected result is buried in sentences and must be re-derived for a test. *Why not Given / When / Then alone:* it hides the arithmetic Kyle checks. *Why not worked bills alone:* they don't say which rule is under test. *Dropped:* May's 20-field scenario record; most of its fields describe tables, and scenarios describe rules. *Cost of changing later:* reformatting text. *Where it lives:* `scenarios/README.md`.
application/DECISION-LOG.md:39:## 2026-11-02 (scenarios) — Ryan: scenarios are written in this repo, in `scenarios/`
application/DECISION-LOG.md:41:- **Option 2 (Ryan, 2026-11-02): scenarios live in tally-utility, in a new `scenarios/` folder; GBM's scenario material is marked superseded and kept as history.** In May the scenario-strategy session put scenarios in GBM, one file per family, inside `invariant-scenarios/`. The source inventory (`application/scenarios-source-inventory-2026-11-02/`) found none of those 29 docs got past the outline of axes, nothing is worked to the cent, and most of the outline predates the rulings since 10-13. *Why:* scenarios sit beside the schema, the parity record and this log, so one commit can tie a scenario to the patch it tests, and they will become tests here; edits while the cash, dispute and collections patches land don't each need a GBM ingestion entry; the old outlines need rewriting anyway, so moving loses little. Kyle has access to this repo, with a Claude session of his own to help him read it. *Why not GBM (option 1):* it splits scenarios from the schema and tests and puts a wiki-ingestion entry on every edit. *Why not the split (option 3, Kyle's batches and answers in GBM):* unnecessary once Kyle reads this repo directly. *Cost of changing later:* a file move. *Where it lives:* `scenarios/` (created with the first scenario); GBM notes and manifest Section BU.
application/DECISION-LOG.md:43:## 2026-11-02 (-28) — rates carry at most four decimals, checked where they are entered
application/DECISION-LOG.md:45:- **Option 2 (Ryan, 2026-11-02): rounding stays the core's (one engine rule, pinned per bill by the snapshot's `formula_version`), and the schema refuses a rate with more than four decimals.** Kyle's K-06: money to the cent, half-up; rates and intermediate math carry four decimals (0.0000); one rule, not per tariff. The check covers the rate a rate item version carries, a schedule's override, and each tier's rate in a tiered item; the columns stay six-decimal, so relaxing it is dropping a check. Factors (meter, heating value, pressure) are not rates and keep six. *Why:* it puts the ruling where a wrong rate is typed, not at billing time; the tables are empty, so nothing is judged after the fact. *Why not leave it all to the core:* a six-decimal rate would be rounded silently or refused far from where it was entered. *Why not per-jurisdiction rounding rows:* Kyle ruled one rule; it builds a choice nobody has. *Accepted:* a jurisdiction that files a five-decimal rate (a per-Mcf gas cost converted to per-Ccf can land there) is refused until the check is relaxed, deliberately. The bill line's own `rate` is the money stream's table: a board note, not this patch. *Where it lives:* v5.4.2-28 section 3.
application/DECISION-LOG.md:47:## 2026-11-02 (-28) — rider dependencies get their own dates, in place, like schedule items
application/DECISION-LOG.md:49:- **Option 1 (Ryan, 2026-11-02): `rate_item_dependencies` becomes bi-temporal in place (A-1 group 2, the `rate_schedule_items` pattern).** In-force dates, recorded-at/recorded-until, frozen once written, correction = close + a row naming what it supersedes, retraction = close as retracted, an as-of read function. -21's retirement (transaction time only, -21 R3) splits into its two meanings: the dependency stopped applying on a date (an end date) or was entered in error (a retraction). The cycle check refuses a cycle among dependencies whose date ranges overlap (conservative: it can refuse links that never coexist on one day; an exact check can come later without a shape change). *Why:* a rider carries its own tariff sheet and effective date, so its basis starts and ends with it, not with a schedule version; the schedule's items are already dated this way, so "which items on a date" and "in what order" answer alike; the table is empty, so the meaning change moves no data. *Why not hang dependencies on the schedule version:* every rider change would need a new schedule version that restates unchanged content, two dating models would describe one tariff, and a retraction has no clean form. Revisit only if Kyle rules the order is fixed by the filed schedule. *Why not leave it to the core:* the parity record grades it a gap, and the core would have no history to read. *Where it lives:* v5.4.2-28 section 2.
application/DECISION-LOG.md:51:## 2026-11-02 (-28) — the meter test fee leaves -28 for its own patch after the dated history
application/DECISION-LOG.md:53:- **Option 1 (Ryan, 2026-11-02): CI-091 moves out of -28.** Kyle's K-06 makes the fee outcome-driven (tolerance and fee as tenant rule data); §7.45(7)(B)(iv) makes a customer-requested test free if none was made for that customer at that location in four years, capped, refunded when the meter is over 2.0% off. Nothing about it is small: charging only on an accurate test needs a fee charge to name its test, and fee charges live in `adhoc_charges` (the money stream's table); the four-year look-back needs who was at the location and where the meter was on a past date, which is the rates stream's dated-history patch (item 6, which waits for -26). *Why:* every piece depends on something not yet built; -28 stays small and reaches review sooner. *Why not the rule table alone now:* a rule nothing checks, whose shape may change once the charge link is designed. *Why not all of it now:* it would rest on the meter history item 6 is about to reshape. *Cost of changing later:* none; no work is lost. *Where it lives:* the rates handoff's list (after dated history); the -12 residuals R1–R3 stand until then.
application/DECISION-LOG.md:55:## 2026-11-02 (-28) — the state-agency check's day boundary is the bill's premise's time zone
application/DECISION-LOG.md:57:- **Option 1 (Ryan, 2026-11-02): the premise of the bill decides the time zone; a consolidated parent uses its children's premises, which must agree.** `customer_is_state_agency_as_of` asked "was this account a state agency at the end of the billed period" at midnight `America/Chicago` (CI-010). It now takes the zone from `premise_time_zone_as_of` for the invoice's premise. A consolidated parent invoice has no premise, so it reads the zones of its children's premises: all must answer and agree, or the check refuses (no children, a premise with no answerable zone, or two zones). *Why:* it is the fix the parity record and -16's comment both named, keeps the change inside the rates stream, and refuses rather than assumes, as the time-zone function already does; a small town's consolidated account sits in one zone in practice. *Why not a utility-wide business-day zone:* nothing stores one today, so it adds a new fact and departs from the places design. *Why not refusing fee lines on a consolidated parent:* that is a rule about how bills are assembled, the money stream's area. *Cost of changing later:* additive. *Where it lives:* v5.4.2-28.
