**K-04.** Neutral internal terms ("sales service" and "transportation service") with a per-jurisdiction label table, and the tenant supplies the exact tariff wording at setup. The pipeline safety fee is an effective-dated rule with amount, basis (per customer or per service line), billing timing, citation and optional recovery-report deadline. Research notes for Texas: a 2009 law raised the per-service-line cap to one dollar, but the current Utilities Code §121.211 text limits total fees to program cost and the Commission sets the amount by rule (16 TAC §8.201), so there is no single fixed cap. One 2024 tariff shows $1.22 per customer, and another requires a recovery report within 90 days after the last billing cycle in which the fee is billed. Confirm the current amount against §8.201 before loading it.

## Wave 7 decisions

**R-11.** Estimation, late fees and program exclusions all become dated rule data with a legal citation.

**K-06.**
- No early-pay discounts, because gas is regulated. Drop that rule type.
- The meter test fee is outcome-driven, with tolerance and fee as rule data, confirmed with the tenant at setup.
- Rounding goes to the cent, and rates and intermediate math carry four decimals (0.0000). Kyle confirmed 2026-10-15: standard rounding to the nearest cent, which can go either up or down (half-up at the midpoint).
- Accessible notice formats: a customer preference field (large print, braille, language) and a record of the format actually sent.

**K-07.** Support municipal utilities with rules entered per utility and no assumed defaults. Not required at launch.
# Kyle's answers: scenario batch 1 (17 questions)

**Date:** 2026-11-03
**Responds to:** Ryan's Slack message of 2026-11-02 (first batch of worked scenarios, `tally-utility/scenarios/`)
**Unblocks:** the expected results of SCN-0001 – SCN-0032 where they depended on these questions

## Standing rule

**Always follow the tariff, including when a city's tariff differs from the platform default.** Platform defaults apply only where the tariff is silent. Where §7.45 binds, a tariff may be stricter than the rule, never looser.

## The seventeen answers

| # | Question | Answer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Rate change partway through a period; gas cost adjustment | **The method (split by days, read date, or cycle) is chosen when the rate change is entered**, with no default, so "refuse until stated" is enforced at setup, not at bill time. **The gas cost factor is set at month end**, applied per billing month, never split, and fixed once locked |
| 2 | Pipeline safety fee | **Added only in the month a city passes it to customers.** A city that absorbs it never shows it on a bill. When billed, the 16 TAC §8.201 limits apply (one-time flat surcharge, not before remittance, at most $1.00 per service line, not to state agencies, report within 90 days of the last cycle billed) |
| 3 | 10% late penalty base | **Default: the current bill only**, charged once, never on prior balances or prior penalties. Tariff wins: Pecan Flat's "10% of the amount due" means the whole amount due |
| 4 | City sales tax on residential gas | **Inside city limits only**, on the lines the tenant flags taxable (per the 2026-10-14 ruling) |
| 5 | Does historical-rate correction (06-12, DE-3) apply to city systems? | **Yes**, as the platform default. The per-target override stays |
| 6 | Straddling bill correction: $57.01 or $55.90? | **$55.90.** A correction repeats the original bill's rate method and changes only what was wrong. "Period end" was shorthand |
| 7 | Read keyed too low | **A human keying error is corrected as a billing correction, even if it adds usage.** The system flags it to the operator, who chooses: (a) keep the keyed read as the prior read and catch up on the next read, or (b) credit-rebill if paid, void-rebill if unpaid. The choice, who made it and why are recorded |
| 8 | Silent ordinance on backbilling | **Split by direction.** Refunds go back as far as the records show. Undercharges default to six months; the tenant may change this by citing its ordinance. A missing rule never holds back a refund |
| 9 | Courtesy credit absorbed by a void | **Carries to the corrected bill** by default. If the credit was given because of the error being corrected, it is queued for operator review |
| 10 | Aging of a corrected bill | **Split.** The amount already owed keeps its original age. Any increase ages from the correction date with a new due date. A decrease reduces the old aged amount |
| 11 | Forfeit-the-straddling-month under a utility's own limit | **Yes**, it applies to the utility's limit too. One rule |
| 12 | Partial payment: backbill or gas first? | **Gas first.** The amounts that can ground a disconnection are paid first; the backbill is paid last |
| 13 | Brenham "also refunded if no bill is past due" | **Two routes only.** (a) After 12 consecutive months of good payment: credited to future bills, or paid by check if the customer asks. (b) At termination: the deposit pays amounts due and the rest is refunded; if it falls short, the customer owes the remainder. "No bill is past due" is a condition of route (a), consistent with §7.45(5)(F)(ii) ("not delinquent in the payment of the current bills") |
| 14 | "No more than two late penalties": window | **Rolling 12 months** |
| 15 | Stinnett extra $125 deposit after a second disconnection | **Tariff wins, so the $125 stands for Pecan Flat.** Modelled as one deposit balance per account that is topped up, not a second deposit. Disconnections counted over a rolling 12 months. Reconnection is not blocked unless the tariff says so. Where §7.45 binds: the total is checked against the one-sixth-of-annual-billings cap, and a disconnection-triggered additional deposit is refused (§7.45(5)(C)(ii) allows an additional deposit only when use runs at least twice the estimate; (5)(F)(i): "no additional deposit may be demanded unless permitted by these rules") |
| 16 | Senior deposit with no DOB on file | **$100.** The customer provides DOB to receive the $40 deposit |
| 17 | Credit left after a deposit-paid final bill is corrected down | **A deposit refund**, always paid out whatever its size |

## Kyle's words (where they shape the build)

> 7. If it's a correction for human error it's just correcting the billing even if it adds usage. It depends on when it's caught and how large the correction is. … flag this to the operator and let them choose the action catch up on next bill or void/credit and rebill. From an audit perspective it puts the control and understanding of the business in the operator's hands.

> 12. Protect the customer, RRC will look on that with favor.

> 15. Always follow the Tariff including if the City tariff is different.

## What this changes

- **Rate changes:** the split method is a required field on a rate change, not a tenant-wide default and not a bill-time refusal.
- **Gas cost adjustment:** one locked factor per billing month; no proration path.
- **Read corrections (Q7):** replace the refusal with an operator decision point. No threshold in the schema. Whichever path is chosen, the next bill's starting read must line up so the missed usage is billed exactly once. The §7.45 law row that lets only rate misapplication travel by reissue needs revisiting for keying errors.
